A||||10||Wade87GT||What happened here ? (long handling question)||||11||
Z||000000||Wade87GT||04-20-2001||10:29 AM||||I'm zooming through a nice open curvy road. A big left 90 degree is coming up so I move to the outside lane, then do a good turn-in to the apex. About halfway through the turn, I'm still a bit on the gas, my Fiero does an abrupt, sort of half s-wiggle. As if something shifted somewhere. I'd thought my heart skipped about 2 beats. Scared the crap out of me! The tires never lost contact and the car continued to stay planted throughout the rest of the turn. There wasn't any sounds and the feeling wasn't "metallic". Also, I wasn't going really fast, just maintained a steady g-load. I've tried to replicate this "shift" during low speed, high-g turns, but it didn't happen. My car: lowered suspension using RCC tubular control arms with poly bushings and coil-over shocks/struts, all springs rated at 300, bigger swaybar in front and big one added to the rear, both poly mounted, Beretta GTZ 16" factory wheels (1991), front tires 225-50-16 and 245-50-16 on the back, all at 35 psi, poly cradle and dogbone bushings, year old eng and trans mounts. Everything is tight with barely noticeable torque steer and no clutch slippage. It does appear that the rear sway bar might be sliding laterally through its cradle mounts, about 1.5". Is it supposed to do this or could it be the problem? Any other ideas?<P>Wade||||reg||11
Z||000001||MrPBody||04-20-2001||11:22 AM||||I really have no clue, so you can skip this ramble if you're looking for a solid answer . . .<P>The anti-roll bar should have no significant lateral load, although in normal operation it may slip from side to side a little; therefore, the fact that it moves laterally either is unimportant or means that the lower control arm is deflecting enough to slide the bar in its bushings.  That would not be good.<P><BR>Other possibilities:<UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>non-obvious pavement irregularity<P><LI>you hit an armadillo<P><LI>you passed through a space-time warp or a gravitational anomaly<P><LI>earthquake (unlikely in Florida)<P><LI>you narrowly avoided falling into a developing sinkhole (quite possible in Florida)<BR></UL><P><BR>I hope it's nothing serious.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||04-20-2001||11:39 AM||||I tend to lean toward the pavement irregularity.<BR>My have been forign material on the road that caused a slip and then the tires regained traction creating the 'S' slip sensation.<BR>Try to duplicate more, if something is wrong it will show eventually||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spektyr||04-20-2001||11:51 AM||||Once is a fluke, twice is a concern, three times is a problem.<P>My philosophy anyway.  I'd say you probably slipped the back end in something on the road.  Just be aware it might happen again so you don't bend the car if it does.<P>Also, there is a very slight toe-in/toe-out shift that occurs when transistioning between acceleration and deceleration in a rear engine rear wheel drive car.  It should be barely noticible, and judging by what you said it's unlikely that it caused this problem.  Just keep in mind that if you back off the throttle in a high g turn, you can produce a bit more oversteer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||04-20-2001||11:59 AM||||Would be nice to know what year your car is. Sounds like you were ridding her close to the edge. I know when I hit that turn from 76E to 476S (long sweeping 5 to 7 degree right bank) at 75mph in my stock 88 it's getting pretty hairy, I can feel every crack and stone in the pavement and it is just as you described (pucker factor 9!). I would never attempt this speed in my 84 as the ass end will surely be leading me through the rest of the turn.||||reg||11
Z||000005||HummelHund||04-20-2001||12:27 PM||||Wade,<BR>I went to Navarre the other day and went around the corner in question both ways, about 45-50mph both times. I didn't feel any pavement irregularity large enough to cause a slip like that. I was driving a Merkur, though, and they tend to soak up bumps and dips better than a Fiero. My dad came through before me in our new Volvo 760 Turbo Wagon (also going around 40-45, I didn't know a wagon could go that fast... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )and didn't notice anything either. Hope it's nothing serious. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||04-20-2001||01:25 PM||||You ran over a manhole cover and lost traction for the split second that the tire contacted the manhole cover. You ran over an oil slick and the same thing happened. <P>Man I don't know!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wade87GT||04-20-2001||01:31 PM||||Smooth road, clear day. I know spring is the migratory and mating season, but no critters around anywhere. I'm starting to believe the "you passed through a space-time warp or a gravitational anomaly" like MrPBody said. I'd like to try it again in the same place, lots of run-off area, 'cept that one pole. I should hook up with the SCCA, they run at an unused airfield in Pensacola.<P>Wade||||reg||2
Z||000008||Fierowrecker||04-20-2001||06:20 PM||||Hey Wade!<BR>Look VERRY closely at the engine cradle...<BR>Look for cracks around the control arm pivot points...<BR>Get an under car wash done to clean up the frame...<BR>Use a magnifying glass!!!<BR>Push, pull and pry everything as the sway bar should not move laterally...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||04-20-2001||06:24 PM||||Perhaps you ran over an old roadkill greezy spot (badger) Or maybe the coconuts in the trunk got loose and are rolling around in the trunk.<P>No really maybe it was just a gust of wind. Ya know wind can play havok on aerodynamics and upset the car  quit a bit.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-20-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||04-22-2001||03:01 PM||||Maybe the tires slipped a little when you crossed the paint stripe.<BR>||||reg||1
